[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Multithreading and Parallel Programming with Task Parallel Library (TPL) in C#"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Task Parallel Library (TPL) is a set of public types and APIs in the System.Threading.Tasks and System.Collections.Concurrent namespaces in .NET. The purpose of the TPL is to make developers more productive by simplifying the process of adding parallelism and concurrency to applications. The TPL scales the degree of concurrency dynamically to most efficiently use all the available processors. In addition, the TPL handles the partitioning of the work, the scheduling of threads on the ThreadPool, thread lifetime management, and other low-level details."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Tasks: Represent asynchronous operations. They are the central abstraction in TPL."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach: Provide simple ways to parallelize loop execution."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Task Scheduler: Manages the execution of tasks."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cancellation Tokens: Allow you to cancel running tasks."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating and Starting Tasks"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The simplest way to create and start a task is using the Task.Run method. This method queues the task to the thread pool and starts it immediately."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Example
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Task task = Task.Run(() =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Task started");
            // Perform some work here
            Console.WriteLine("Task completed");
        });

        task.Wait(); // Wait for the task to complete
        Console.WriteLine("Main thread continues");
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Parallel Loops (Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Parallel.For and Parallel.ForEach methods provide a simple way to parallelize loop execution. The TPL automatically partitions the data and schedules the work across multiple threads."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Example
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Parallel.For
        Parallel.For(0, 10, i =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Iteration {i} executed by thread {System.Threading.Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId}");
            // Perform some work here
        });

        // Parallel.ForEach
        string[] data = { "item1", "item2", "item3", "item4", "item5" };
        Parallel.ForEach(data, item =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Processing {item} by thread {System.Threading.Thread.CurrentThread.ManagedThreadId}");
            // Perform some work here
        });

        Console.WriteLine("Parallel loops completed");
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Task Cancellation"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The CancellationTokenSource and CancellationToken classes allow you to cancel running tasks. You can create a CancellationTokenSource, obtain its CancellationToken, and pass the token to the task. When you want to cancel the task, call the CancellationTokenSource.Cancel method."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Example
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        CancellationTokenSource cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        CancellationToken token = cts.Token;

        Task task = Task.Run(() =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Task started");
            for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
            {
                if (token.IsCancellationRequested)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("Task cancelled");
                    return;
                }
                Console.WriteLine($"Iteration {i}");
                Thread.Sleep(100); // Simulate some work
            }
            Console.WriteLine("Task completed");
        }, token);

        Console.WriteLine("Press any key to cancel the task");
        Console.ReadKey();
        cts.Cancel(); // Cancel the task

        try
        {
            task.Wait();
        }
        catch (AggregateException ae)
        {
            foreach (var e in ae.InnerExceptions)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Exception: {e.GetType().Name}");
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine("Main thread continues");
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Task Continuations"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Task continuations allow you to specify code that should run after a task completes. You can use the ContinueWith method to create a continuation task."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Example
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Task<int> task = Task.Run(() =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Task started");
            // Perform some work here
            return 42;
        });

        Task continuation = task.ContinueWith(antecedent =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Task completed with result: {antecedent.Result}");
        });

        continuation.Wait();
        Console.WriteLine("Main thread continues");
    }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Exception Handling"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a task throws an exception, the exception is wrapped in an AggregateException. You can catch the AggregateException and iterate through its InnerExceptions to handle the individual exceptions."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class Example
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Task task = Task.Run(() =>
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Task started");
            throw new Exception("Something went wrong");
        });

        try
        {
            task.Wait();
        }
        catch (AggregateException ae)
        {
            foreach (var e in ae.InnerExceptions)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Exception: {e.Message}");
            }
        }

        Console.WriteLine("Main thread continues");
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Task Parallel Library (TPL) provides a powerful and convenient way to add parallelism and concurrency to your C# applications. By using tasks, parallel loops, cancellation tokens, and continuations, you can write efficient and responsive applications that take full advantage of multi-core processors."}]
